=====================================================================
FINDINGS THAT DO NOT BLOCK, BUT THE LEAD SHOULD SEE
=====================================================================
(A) THE ORACLE'S NEW CHECK 3b IS WEAKER THAN IT LOOKS -- it can be satisfied by
a recipe that enforces nothing. This is my answer to the question the lead put
to me about the third amendment.

  Faithfulness: the SHAPE is faithful. It asserts the four named targets and
  asserts the negative (caching not disabled suite-wide), which is exactly the
  two halves of user decision 2026-08-20h. Using `make -n` rather than grepping
  the Makefile text is a real improvement over the scanning machinery this
  oracle previously carried, because it asks make what it would actually run,
  which follows variables, includes and prerequisite recipes.

  Strength: NOT sufficient on its own. `make -n` prints the command with the
  ignore-errors prefix STRIPPED, and the regex `test[^|]*-count=1[^|]*internal/
  handlers/accounts` only excludes a pipe BETWEEN "test" and the package path,
  not after it. I built two throwaway Makefiles and ran the oracle's exact grep
  against them:
    recipe `-$(GO) test -count=1 $(ACCOUNTS_PKG)`        -> MATCH (3b PASSes)
       `make -n` prints it as "... go test -count=1 ./internal/handlers/accounts",
       the leading `-` invisible; at run time make would IGNORE the failure.
    recipe `$(GO) test -count=1 $(ACCOUNTS_PKG) || true` -> MATCH (3b PASSes)
  Both are decorative and both pass check 3b. It also cannot see `.IGNORE:`,
  a `-k` in MAKEFLAGS, or `.ONESHELL`. The negative check
  (`grep -qE 'test .*-count=1 \./\.\.\.'`) is likewise evadable by
  `GOFLAGS=-count=1`, a preceding `go clean -testcache`, or
  `-count=1 ./cmd/... ./internal/...`.

  Consequence for this attempt: none, but only because I did not rely on the
  oracle for it. Item 2 rules out all of those constructs by inspection AND item
  1 demonstrates the behaviour end to end -- make actually aborts, at the added
  line, with rc 2, in six targets. If a future attempt changes these recipes,
  the oracle alone will not catch a `-` prefix or a trailing `|| true`; the
  behavioural test (delete a symlinked node, hold PATH constant, run the target,
  expect nonzero) is the check that has teeth. My recommendation, for the lead
  to accept or ignore: if 3b is amended again, replace the `make -n` grep with
  that behavioural run. I did not edit accept.sh.

(B) ONE COST FIGURE IN THE MAKEFILE COMMENT IS UNDERSTATED FOR `race`. The
comment says "that package takes well under a second, so the cost is
negligible", covering all four rerun lines. Measured with node present and a
warm build cache: plain reruns 0.46s / 0.55s wall -- accurate; the `race` rerun
`go test -race -count=1 ./internal/handlers/accounts` is 3.30s / 3.31s wall
(test binary ~2.99s), i.e. roughly 3x "well under a second". The conclusion
("the cost is negligible") still holds -- `make race` runs a 244s -race suite
ahead of it -- and no acceptance criterion turns on the number, so I am
recording it rather than failing on it.

(C) CARRIED FORWARD FROM ATTEMPT 7, STILL TRUE, STILL OUT OF SCOPE: the harness
JS header comment (unchanged since attempt 5) says "a machine without node
cannot silently skip it" with no pointer to the cache caveat, though it does
direct the reader to the test file where the caveat now lives. Not a false
statement about the test's behaviour; attempt 8 was not asked to touch that file
and did not (md5 3ea4ad74 unchanged).
